      <h2>Why pasting Markdown into rich-text editors usually breaks</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Plain-text clipboard vs. HTML clipboard MIME types:</strong> When you press <code>Cmd/Ctrl+C</code> inside a source code editor like VS Code, the clipboard only receives <code>text/plain</code>. Rich-text editors like Confluence, Google Docs, or email clients treat it as literal paragraphs full of <code>###</code> and backticks instead of structured headings and tables.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Malformed tables &amp; citation noise from LLMs:</strong> Copying answers from ChatGPT, Claude, or DeepSeek often brings invisible zero-width spaces, citation tags (<code>【†source】</code>), and GFM tables whose <code>|---|---|</code> separator row has one too few columns — causing web parsers to drop the entire table.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Local image paths cannot cross into SaaS editors:</strong> A Markdown link like <code>![](_assets/arch-diagram.png)</code> points to your local disk. Pasting that document into Notion, Confluence, Medium, or Ghost leaves broken image placeholders unless images are hosted on HTTPS URLs or exported in a self-contained format.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h2>How SoloMD’s Rich-Copy &amp; Image Hosting workflow solves it</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Copy as Rich HTML with theme-accurate code &amp; table styles:</strong> SoloMD writes clean, inline-styled <code>text/html</code> alongside <code>text/plain</code> to your system clipboard. Code blocks keep readable monospace shading and line structure, lists keep proper indentation, and LaTeX math converts cleanly for Word and rich-text targets.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>5 built-in image hosting backends with automatic offline fallback:</strong> Configure <strong>Cloudflare R2 / AWS S3 / MinIO</strong>, <strong>GitHub repository + CDN</strong>, <strong>PicGo</strong>, <strong>SM.MS</strong>, or any custom upload script in Settings → Images. Paste a screenshot into SoloMD and it uploads immediately, inserting the HTTPS URL. If you are on a plane with no Wi-Fi, SoloMD automatically saves to local <code>_assets/</code> instead of losing your screenshot.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Clean AI Artifacts before publishing:</strong> One toolbar click removes LLM citation clutter and repairs broken Markdown table delimiters on the fly. You can also edit in <strong>Pure WYSIWYG mode</strong> so syntax markers stay hidden even on the active line.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Webhook extensions &amp; 9-format Export Center:</strong> Need to push directly to an internal CMS API or hand off a file? Use SoloMD’s declarative JSON extensions (<code>.solomd/plugins/*.json</code>) for custom Webhook publishing, or use the built-in 9-format Export Center (DOCX, PDF, standalone searchable HTML, EPUB, ODT, RTF, LaTeX, PNG, TextBundle).
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>When another tool may still fit better</h2>
      <p>
        Honest engineering trade-offs matter: if your team collaborates simultaneously with live multi-cursor comments inside a single document, draft directly in <strong>Notion, Google Docs, or Confluence</strong> rather than round-tripping Markdown. And if you design pixel-custom magazine layouts with animated SVG blocks for social media feeds, a specialized visual layout studio will still be your final step after drafting prose in SoloMD.
      </p>

